  "expected_gain": {
    "point": 0.18379,
    "unit": "expected responses per week",
    "caveat": "Scales linearly with base_response_rate (a your-input number) and is computed from an observational proxy. Read the interval, not this number.",
    "ci80": [
      0.18543,
      0.2032
    ],
    "p_positive": 1.0,
    "straddles_zero": false,
    "verdict": "The gain is positive in 100.0% of draws and the 80% interval [+0.185, +0.203] excludes zero \u2014 under the model's own assumptions. That is not the same as being right about the world.",
    "optimizers_curse": {
      "what_it_is": "If the allocation is re-optimised inside every Monte Carlo draw, it adapts to noise nobody can observe, and the reported gain flatters the method rather than describing the decision. Both quantities are computed here so the difference is visible instead of accidental.",
      "fixed_proposal_mean_gain": 0.19433,
      "reoptimised_mean_gain": 0.19447,
      "optimism": 0.00013,
      "optimism_share_of_reported_gain": 0.0007,
      "reoptimised_ci80": [
        0.18566,
        0.20324
      ],
      "why_it_matters": "The re-optimised figure is the one a naive Monte Carlo would print. Any gap between it and the fixed-proposal figure is gain that exists only because the optimiser was allowed to see the noise."
    },
    "point_outside_interval": true,
    "point_vs_interval_note": "The point estimate sits OUTSIDE its own interval, which looks like a bug and is a finding. The point estimate fixes the role_quality weight at Chapter 11's 0.0 \u2014 the value that makes the role-quality signal irrelevant. The Monte Carlo draws it from U[0, 0.2] because the book never pinned it, and the firms this engine allocates to tend to pay above the BLS median, so almost every draw with a non-zero weight scores them higher. The interval is therefore not centred on the point estimate: it is telling you that the recommendation's value depends on a parameter nobody has decided."
  },
  "what_to_check_later": "Responses per company against the composite that earned each slot. If the top-composite firms do not out-respond the rest, the engine is optimising a correlation that does not survive the intervention \u2014 which is the open question the causal section says it cannot answer yet."
